🚀 Week 4 – Day 5 Stack vs Heap & JVM Memory Areas ☕

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🧠 Introduction

Java programs use different memory areas inside the JVM.

👉 Understanding memory structure helps in:

✔ Performance optimization
✔ Debugging memory issues
✔ Writing efficient programs

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🏗️ 1. JVM Memory Areas

The JVM mainly contains:

1️⃣ Heap Memory
2️⃣ Stack Memory
3️⃣ Method Area
4️⃣ PC Register
5️⃣ Native Method Stack

---

📊 JVM Memory Structure

```text id="j1"
JVM Memory
│
├── Heap Memory
├── Stack Memory
├── Method Area
├── PC Register
└── Native Method Stack
```

---

🔹 2. Stack Memory

Stack memory stores:

✔ Local variables
✔ Method calls
✔ References

---

📌 Example

```java id="s1"
void test() {
    int x = 10;
}
```

👉 `x` is stored in stack memory.

---

🔍 Features of Stack Memory

✔ Thread-specific
✔ Fast memory access
✔ Automatically cleared after method execution

---

⚠️ StackOverflowError

Occurs due to excessive recursion.

```java id="s2"
void recursion() {
    recursion();
}
```

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 3. Heap Memory

Heap memory stores:

✔ Objects
✔ Instance variables

---

📌 Example

```java id="h1"
Student s = new Student();
```

👉 Object is stored in heap memory.

---

🔍 Features of Heap Memory

✔ Shared among threads
✔ Managed by Garbage Collector
✔ Larger memory area

---

⚠️ OutOfMemoryError

Occurs when heap memory is full.

---

📊 Stack vs Heap

| Feature         | Stack Memory      | Heap Memory       |
| --------------- | ----------------- | ----------------- |
| Stores          | Local variables   | Objects           |
| Access Speed    | Faster            | Slower            |
| Memory Size     | Smaller           | Larger            |
| Thread Specific | ✔ Yes             | ❌ No              |
| Managed By      | JVM automatically | Garbage Collector |

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 4. Method Area

Stores:

✔ Class metadata
✔ Static variables
✔ Method information

---

📌 Example

```java id="m1"
static int count = 0;
```

---

🔹 5. PC Register

Stores address of current JVM instruction for each thread.

✔ Helps JVM track execution flow.

---

🔹 6. Native Method Stack

Used for native methods written in:

* C
* C++

---

♻️ Garbage Collection & Heap

Garbage Collector removes unused objects from heap memory.

---

📌 Example

```java id="g1"
Student s = new Student();
s = null;
```

👉 Object becomes eligible for garbage collection.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🎯 Why Memory Understanding Matters?

✔ Improves coding efficiency
✔ Helps optimize applications
✔ Important for interviews

---

⚠️ Common Mistakes

❌ Creating unnecessary objects
❌ Infinite recursion
❌ Memory leaks due to unused references

---

🧠 Real Insight

👉 Most Java performance issues are related to:

* Heap memory usage
* Object creation
* Garbage collection

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📚 Key Takeaways

✔ Stack stores local variables and method calls
✔ Heap stores objects
✔ Method area stores class-level data
✔ Garbage Collector manages heap memory
✔ JVM contains multiple memory areas

